1️⃣ What is CallableStatement?
CallableStatement is a JDBC interface in Java.
It is used to call stored procedures in the database.

Stored procedures can have:
IN parameters → input to procedure
OUT parameters → output from procedure
INOUT parameters → both input & output

Example:
CallableStatement cs = con.prepareCall("{call procedure_name(?, ?)}");

2️⃣ Why use CallableStatement?
Code Reusability: Complex SQL logic can be written once in the database and reused.
Performance: Stored procedures run on the database server → faster than multiple SQL queries from Java.
Security: Helps prevent SQL injection when using parameters.
Easier Maintenance: SQL logic is in the database, Java code is cleaner.

3️⃣ Difference between Statement, PreparedStatement, and CallableStatement
Feature	          Statement	                  PreparedStatement	                    CallableStatement
Purpose	       Execute simple SQL queries	  Execute parameterized SQL queries	   Execute stored procedures
Parameters	   No parameters	              Input parameters only	               IN, OUT, INOUT parameters
Security	   Low (prone to SQL injection)	  Higher (precompiled queries)         Higher (parameters safely handled)
Performance	   Less efficient	              More efficient (precompiled)	       Efficient for complex procedures
Example	      stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM Employee")	ps.setInt(1, 10);      ps.executeQuery()	cs.setInt(1, 10); cs.registerOutParameter(2, Types.INTEGER); cs.execute()

💡 Summary:
Statement → simple queries
PreparedStatement → parameterized queries
CallableStatement → stored procedures